WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.976
Picture a group chat that sorts messages by the time on the phone that sent each one.

2
00:00:08.233 --> 00:00:10.228
Ana asks: dinner at eight?

3
00:00:10.228 --> 00:00:12.684
Ben answers: yes, see you there!

4
00:00:12.967 --> 00:00:16.228
But on the screen, the answer sits above the question.

5
00:00:16.228 --> 00:00:21.300
Ben's phone runs a minute slow, so his answer got an earlier time than her question.

6
00:00:21.533 --> 00:00:23.108
So here's the real question.

7
00:00:23.108 --> 00:00:28.224
If no two clocks agree, how do you put things in the order they could really have happened?

8
00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:30.679
In a chat, that's a funny moment.

9
00:00:30.679 --> 00:00:32.198
With money, it's a bug.

10
00:00:32.198 --> 00:00:35.566
Take a card payment in two steps, on two computers.

11
00:00:35.566 --> 00:00:37.679
We'll call each computer a node.

12
00:00:37.967 --> 00:00:44.267
Auth approves a payment and sends a message to capture, which moves the money 5 milliseconds later.

13
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.958
Auth's clock runs 40 milliseconds ahead.

14
00:00:46.958 --> 00:00:53.409
So the approval is stamped a hundred and forty milliseconds past ten, and the capture a hundred and five.

15
00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:59.979
Sort by those stamps, and the money moves 35 milliseconds before the payment was even approved.

16
00:01:00.267 --> 00:01:02.484
The obvious fix is better clocks.

17
00:01:02.484 --> 00:01:10.950
But NTP, the service that keeps computer clocks close to the right time, can easily leave 40 milliseconds between two servers.

18
00:01:10.950 --> 00:01:13.705
And a message often takes less than that.

19
00:01:13.933 --> 00:01:20.187
Whenever the gap between two clocks is bigger than the trip, sorting by time can flip a cause and its effect.

20
00:01:20.187 --> 00:01:24.720
Wouldn't it be nice to have an order that stays right, whatever the clocks say?

21
00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:27.237
So what order do we actually need?

22
00:01:27.237 --> 00:01:28.553
Not the time of day.

23
00:01:28.553 --> 00:01:30.396
Causes before their effects.

24
00:01:30.633 --> 00:01:33.754
And a cause can reach an effect in only two ways.

25
00:01:33.754 --> 00:01:36.748
Along one node's own events, one after another.

26
00:01:36.748 --> 00:01:39.869
Or along a message, from the send to its arrival.

27
00:01:40.133 --> 00:01:46.007
So if a stamp only ever grows along both, an effect can never get a smaller stamp than its cause.

28
00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:49.105
People can do this with no clock at all.

29
00:01:49.105 --> 00:01:50.212
Back to the chat.

30
00:01:50.212 --> 00:01:56.333
Ana and Ben stop trusting their phones, and each keeps a tally instead, a number on a notepad.

31
00:01:56.600 --> 00:02:01.856
Before Ana sends anything, she adds one to her tally, and writes it on the message.

32
00:02:01.856 --> 00:02:03.312
Her question carries 1.

33
00:02:03.533 --> 00:02:06.542
When Ben reads a message, he looks at its number.

34
00:02:06.542 --> 00:02:10.533
He takes the bigger of his tally and the message's, and adds one.

35
00:02:10.533 --> 00:02:14.156
His tally was 0, the message says 1, so the read becomes 2.

36
00:02:14.400 --> 00:02:16.317
Then his answer carries 3.

37
00:02:16.317 --> 00:02:18.971
Sort by tally: question 1, answer 3.

38
00:02:18.971 --> 00:02:21.993
The right order, and nobody read a clock.

39
00:02:22.233 --> 00:02:25.062
Ben's phone can stay a minute slow forever.

40
00:02:25.062 --> 00:02:26.641
Nobody asks it any more.

41
00:02:27.033 --> 00:02:27.873
Let's map it.

42
00:02:27.873 --> 00:02:29.293
Each friend is a node.

43
00:02:29.293 --> 00:02:30.778
The tally is a counter.

44
00:02:30.778 --> 00:02:34.006
The number on the message is the stamp it carries.

45
00:02:34.006 --> 00:02:38.267
And the bigger of the two, plus one, is the jump past the message.

46
00:02:38.467 --> 00:02:39.474
Now three nodes.

47
00:02:39.474 --> 00:02:44.697
Auth approves payments, capture moves the money, and the ledger keeps the balances.

48
00:02:44.697 --> 00:02:47.340
Each counts its own events, starting at 1.

49
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:49.344
Auth approves, and gets 1.

50
00:02:49.344 --> 00:02:52.831
Capture and the ledger are busy with other payments.

51
00:02:52.831 --> 00:02:55.983
Capture is at 1, and the ledger counts 1, 2, 3.

52
00:02:56.267 --> 00:02:59.885
Auth counts the send as 2, and the message carries 2.

53
00:02:59.885 --> 00:03:01.592
Capture's counter says 1.

54
00:03:01.592 --> 00:03:03.777
What does capture's receive get?

55
00:03:03.777 --> 00:03:04.869
Pause and guess.

56
00:03:08.133 --> 00:03:08.285
3.

57
00:03:08.285 --> 00:03:11.095
The bigger of 1 and 2 is 2, plus one.

58
00:03:11.095 --> 00:03:15.727
Everything capture does from now on sorts after the approval.

59
00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:18.539
Capture sends to the ledger, with 4.

60
00:03:18.539 --> 00:03:21.572
But the ledger has counted to 5 on its own.

61
00:03:21.572 --> 00:03:25.663
So the receive becomes the bigger of 5 and 4, plus one: 6.

62
00:03:25.933 --> 00:03:27.281
Notice what happened.

63
00:03:27.281 --> 00:03:30.812
A receive always ends up above the send that caused it.

64
00:03:30.812 --> 00:03:32.545
That's the whole guarantee.

65
00:03:32.800 --> 00:03:38.862
This is called a Lamport timestamp, after Leslie Lamport, who described it in 1978.

66
00:03:38.862 --> 00:03:42.733
And could have caused has a name too: happens before.

67
00:03:43.000 --> 00:03:49.065
A stamp is the counter plus the node's own number, so a tie breaks the same way everywhere.

68
00:03:49.065 --> 00:03:51.397
Auth is 0, capture 1, the ledger 2.

69
00:03:51.667 --> 00:03:58.517
Sort all eleven stamps by counter, then by node number, and every event gets one place in one list.

70
00:03:58.517 --> 00:04:00.939
Every cause sits before its effect.

71
00:04:01.233 --> 00:04:04.601
So, does a smaller stamp mean it happened first?

72
00:04:04.601 --> 00:04:05.583
It's tempting.

73
00:04:05.583 --> 00:04:06.565
Let's test it.

74
00:04:06.833 --> 00:04:08.143
Auth's send has 2.

75
00:04:08.143 --> 00:04:10.400
The ledger's third event has 3.

76
00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:11.491
Two is smaller.

77
00:04:11.491 --> 00:04:13.092
But follow the arrows.

78
00:04:13.092 --> 00:04:17.896
The only path from auth reaches the ledger at 6, long after its 3.

79
00:04:18.167 --> 00:04:20.464
Neither could have known about the other.

80
00:04:20.464 --> 00:04:24.217
They're concurrent, and the order between them is only a tie-break.

81
00:04:24.500 --> 00:04:26.490
So the rule runs one way only.

82
00:04:26.490 --> 00:04:28.878
If a caused b, a's stamp is smaller.

83
00:04:28.878 --> 00:04:33.390
A smaller stamp doesn't mean a caused b, or even that it came first.

84
00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:38.564
Telling those apart takes vector clocks: one counter per node, in every stamp.

85
00:04:38.800 --> 00:04:41.452
And a counter has no idea what time it is.

86
00:04:41.452 --> 00:04:44.104
It's great at after, and hopeless at when.

87
00:04:44.104 --> 00:04:48.335
Ask it for everything after ten o'clock, and it just stares at you.

88
00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:53.846
A quiet node counts slowly, so its counter can drift thousands away from a busy one's.

89
00:04:53.846 --> 00:04:57.079
Use these stamps when you need causes before effects.

90
00:04:57.079 --> 00:05:03.361
When you also need the time of day, the clock has to come back in, carefully, and that's the next step.

91
00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:04.422
So, the chat.

92
00:05:04.422 --> 00:05:09.480
Count events instead of reading clocks, and jump past every message you receive.

93
00:05:09.480 --> 00:05:14.159
Then an answer can never sort above its question, whatever the phones say.

94
00:05:14.433 --> 00:05:15.560
One quick check.

95
00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:19.784
Capture's counter is at 9, and a message arrives carrying 4.

96
00:05:19.784 --> 00:05:22.037
What stamp does the receive get?

97
00:05:22.037 --> 00:05:23.164
Pause and think.

98
00:05:26.333 --> 00:05:26.624
10.

99
00:05:26.624 --> 00:05:29.729
The bigger of 9 and 4, plus one.

100
00:05:29.729 --> 00:05:30.311
Not 5.

101
00:05:30.311 --> 00:05:32.931
The jump only ever goes up.
